                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U: D \to Set
            is monadic. For example, classical Stone duality is the case where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is the category of Stone spaces,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            is the category of Boolean algebras, and the Boolean operations on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{2}
            are continuous with respect to its Stone space structure, making
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{2}
            a Boolean algebra object in the category of Stone spaces. (For much more on this, see Johnstone’s classic treatise
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stone+Spaces"} Stone Spaces
          %p The point is that in each of these situations, a Stone duality is a restriction of the more global duality on Chu spaces, in that the diagram
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
